A renderer with strictly less authority, which is the sandbox this
platform has.

Patch 0047 turned two functions into refusals and wrote down, in both of
them, exactly what would have to become true before they could answer
anything else:

    The condition for changing both is nameable: the launcher spawning a
    renderer with strictly less authority than itself, and these functions
    being able to ask what this process was given.

M165 built the launcher half - /bin/chromiumcontent spawns a second process
and //content dispatches it as a renderer - and this is the rest of it.

WHAT THE SANDBOX IS HERE

There is no seccomp-bpf and there are no namespaces. What there is is a
capability set the kernel assigns from a manifest at spawn, which can only
ever shrink, and which since M166 belongs to the PROCESS rather than to the
thread that asks. A process holding none of it cannot map the screen, read
the clipboard, list processes, open a socket, write a file or power the
machine off; the kernel refuses each of those by name.

So entering the sandbox is giving the whole set up, and being in it is
holding nothing. Sandbox::EnterCapabilitySandbox() is the first and
Sandbox::IsProcessSandboxed() reads back the second - which is why the
implementation returns what sys_dropcaps() reports rather than true: the
answer is the measurement, and a caller that wants to check rather than
trust can call sys_getcaps() itself.

WHY THE RENDERER AND NOT THE LAUNCHER

RendererMainPlatformDelegate::EnableSandbox() is where Chromium puts this on
every platform with a sandbox of its own, and the reason is the same here:
RendererMain calls it before the message loop runs and therefore before
anything at all has arrived over the pipe from the browser. Everything a
renderer does with somebody else's bytes is on the far side of that call.
The process about to run that code is the one that closes the door.

A renderer needs nothing from the set. The pipe it talks to the browser over
is a descriptor it was handed, and this platform's Unix-domain sockets,
shared memory and futexes were built to need no capability - M118 says so in
the commit that built them, and gives this as the reason.

//content/renderer NOW DEPENDS ON //sandbox/policy

On Linux that dependency is reached through use_seccomp_bpf, which is false
here, so //sandbox/policy was in no binary and sandbox::policy::Sandbox was
in no binary either. A declaration is not a definition: without the dep the
renderer compiles and the program does not link, which is M164's sentence
arriving a third time. Adding it drags nothing - the sandbox's Linux half is
already behind has_linux_kernel from patch 0047, and the target builds for
this platform with no further change.

